The '''Great Adonian War period (1935–1947)''' covers Guajicutea's involvement in the [[Great Adonian War]]. The island served initially as a staging ground for the Volisanian invasion of [[Zong]] [[Balboa]], became a front line as the war shifted northward, was reduced to a Volisanian military territory in 1943, and was liberated following the overthrow of the [[National Volisan Party|NVP]] in [[Volisania]] in 1947. A domestic resistance movement, the [[Grupo de Liberación Guajicuteano]] (GLG), funded covertly by Zong, waged a campaign of sabotage and bombing from the island's volcanic tunnels throughout the war. The era ended on July 3, 1947, when the last Volisanian forces withdrew from the island.

== Staging ground (1935–1936) ==

The [[Great Adonian War]] opened in 1935 with the Balboa campaign, in which [[Volisania|Volisanian]] and Balboan forces invaded Zong Balboa. Guajicutea, with [[Waribá]] port leased to Volisania and a Volisanian military base in operation since 1930, functioned as a staging ground for the invasion. Volisanian naval forces used the island as a stopover point for refuelling and concealment. The island's technical independence — neither Volisania nor Sarta — made it a safe harbour for naval operations, as it had been for war production in the preceding period.

== The Grupo de Liberación Guajicuteano ==

A covert resistance network, the [[Grupo de Liberación Guajicuteano]] (GLG), was formed in 1935–1936 with funding from [[Zong]]. The GLG conducted attacks on political and military infrastructure. The organisation was initially small but expanded as the regime's repression intensified. The network's cellular structure made it difficult for the government to identify members, and the inability to distinguish operatives from civilians contributed to increasingly broad crackdowns on civil life.

== Crackdowns on civil life (1935–1936) ==

The regime responded to GLG activity with widespread repression. Curfews were imposed. A ban on "unemployment" effectively conscripted anyone without employment into war production or military support service. The government's inability to distinguish between civilians and GLG operatives resulted in indiscriminate crackdowns.

== Racial segregation and internment (1936) ==

In 1936, the regime enacted the first racial segregation laws and established the [[Campo de Las Minas]], a concentration camp in the Bukan district of the northern highlands, outside [[Waribá]]. The entire Zong population of Guajicutea was interned at the site, a former mining area in a remote, mountainous district.

In the same year, privacy protection laws were overturned, permitting police to enter homes without cause or warrant. The internment of the Zong population marked a reversal of the position taken in the 1931 memorandum, in which Guajicutea's fascist leadership had advised Volisania against implementing racial laws on the grounds that the mixed population was essential to war production. The onset of direct hostilities with Zong provided the regime with a justification for measures it had previously resisted.

== The slow stripping of rights (1936–1941) ==

Over the five years following the 1936 segregation laws, civil rights were incrementally removed. The domestic population remained divided in its assessment of the GLG: employment was high and most people were economically secure, while the GLG's attacks on infrastructure were disruptive. Some regarded the GLG as a resistance movement; others viewed it as a destabilising force. The regime maintained public acquiescence through economic prosperity while gradually reducing freedoms.

== Zong bombing campaign (1941) ==

On April 1, 1941, [[Zong]] bombers attacked the port of [[Waribá]], producing widespread panic. The bombing continued with biweekly raids on Volisanian military infrastructure on the island. The campaign ended the perception of Guajicutea as a safe harbour beyond the reach of the war.

== Protests and the Matanza de Tabakurí (1941) ==

The bombing prompted street protests demanding that the government end the war. The government was not in a position to do so, as the island was a Volisanian military staging ground. The regime responded to the protests with further crackdowns. During this period, the '''[[Matanza de Tabakurí]]''' took place: state forces killed protesters in the capital. The massacre shifted public sentiment toward the GLG, whose popularity increased as the regime's violence escalated from political repression to the killing of civilians.

== The camp riot (October 1941) ==

In October 1941, a "riot" occurred at the [[Campo de Las Minas]] internment camp. The event was most likely staged: many Zong internees were killed by the regime and the deaths were presented as the result of a prisoner uprising. News of the killings reached the local community around Waribá, and a crowd forced its way into the camp to free the internees. Those who participated in the break-in were subsequently arrested and tried. The incident exposed both the regime's brutality and the public's increasing willingness to resist.

== Guajicutea becomes the front line (1942) ==

In 1942, [[Zong]] and [[Sinchew]] forces pushed back the northern Balboa campaign, driving Balboan and Volisanian forces northward toward [[Tapuya]] and its islands. Guajicutea became part of the front line. The island, now within the combat zone, was subjected to intensified bombing.

== Relinquishment of sovereignty (February 8, 1943) ==

On February 8, 1943, the Guajicutean government relinquished control to the Volisanian military. The country became, in effect, a territory of Volisania. President [[Ernesto Guerrero]] and [[Jefe de la Asamblea]] [[Damián Cordero]] were reduced to administrative functions under Volisanian military command. The relinquishment provoked mass rioting, which was suppressed by Volisanian forces.

== GLG bombing campaign and the Battles of the Caves (1943–1944) ==

Following the relinquishment, the GLG escalated its bombing campaign, targeting vehicles, government buildings, and military infrastructure. GLG operatives operated from the volcanic tunnels of Guajicutea, where the island's geology provided a network of caves and lava tubes suited to concealment, weapons storage, and movement between positions.

The Volisanian military conducted a campaign against the GLG in the tunnel systems, resulting in the '''[[Battles of the Caves]]''' (1943–1944), a series of engagements in the volcanic highlands. The Volisanian military, trained for conventional warfare, encountered difficulties in the subterranean environment. The campaign concluded with the capture of the GLG leadership — political leader [[Tomás Yabarí]] and military commander [[Augusto Rendón]] — in 1944. The capture ended the GLG's organised resistance.

== The superfortress and the Battle of Waribá (1945) ==

In 1945, Zong forces attempted to capture islands along the Tapuyan coast and advance toward Guajicutea. The offensive led to a concentration of Volisanian forces on the island, which became the most heavily fortified position in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]].

The '''[[Battle of Waribá]]''' was the principal air battle of the war on Guajicutean soil. Zong forces firebombed the city of Waribá, causing extensive destruction. The battle established that Guajicutea had become a primary military target rather than a rear-area staging ground.

== Decampment and famine (1946) ==

In 1946, Volisanian forces withdrew from Guajicutea to redirect the fighting to the mainland in the Tapuya campaign. The withdrawal ended Volisanian funding for the war production economy. The factories and infrastructure built to serve the Volisanian military lost their source of support. Workers were laid off, food imports ceased, and the economy oriented toward military production collapsed. A famine began: agriculture had been neglected during the war production boom, and the food import networks had been Volisanian military supply chains that were dismantled with the withdrawal.

== Liberation and reconstruction (1947–1948) ==

In 1947, the [[National Volisan Party|NVP]] was overthrown by liberal revolutionaries in [[Volisania]]. The collapse of the fascist government in Volisania compelled the withdrawal of all remaining Volisanian forces from Guajicutea. On July 3, 1947, the last Volisanian forces departed the island. Guajicutea's liberation resulted from the political transformation of Volisania rather than from a domestic military victory.

A group of conservative-moderate leaders who had been retained during the war — referred to as the rump government — assumed governance following the withdrawal. Weeks of domestic unrest followed, including political violence and food shortages. On August 7, 1947, the rump government requested a coalition occupation by [[Balisca|Baliscan]] forces to restore order. Baliscan forces occupied the island for six months, during which they worked to restore civil order, release political prisoners from the [[Campo de Las Minas]] and other detention facilities, and re-establish food supply. [[Asikyira]] sent aid during the occupation, including food, medical supplies, and reconstruction assistance.

On December 18, 1947, a new constitution was promulgated, establishing the '''Third Republic of Guajicutea''' and instituting the modern nation. On January 1, 1948, the [[Adonian Community]] was created to prevent future conflicts, and Guajicutea was invited to be an initial member. The Baliscan occupation concluded in February 1948.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 430,000 in 1935 to approximately 453,000 by 1947, a growth rate of approximately 0.4% per year. The rate was reduced by the war, the 1946 famine, and general conditions of repression. Precise casualty figures are uncertain.

